<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peasants' Revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1381">1381</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Wat Tyler's Rebellion, Great Rising of 1381</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peasants' Revolt of 1381 was the largest popular rising in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Triggered by the third poll tax in four years, and fed by resentment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> and of the laws holding down wages after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>, rebels from Essex and Kent marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in June 1381, burned the Savoy Palace and killed the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>. The young Richard II met them and promised reforms, but after their leader Wat Tyler was killed at Smithfield the rising was put down and the promises were revoked.</p></div>
